1
00:00:04,217 --> 00:00:07,470
Before the law, a word about words.

2
00:00:09,020 --> 00:00:20,087
Take the word fit. A key fits a lock. A coat fits a man. A man is fit for a job, or
he is not.

3
00:00:20,137 --> 00:00:29,343
A pilot whose eyes fail is unfit to fly, and nobody hears that and thinks the pilot
is unfit to live.

4
00:00:29,393 --> 00:00:46,044
That is because fit always asks a question with two parts: fit for what? A key that
will not open this lock is not a bad key. It is the wrong key for this door.

5
00:00:46,094 --> 00:00:58,936
A pilot who can no longer fly is still the same man he was yesterday. He is still a
husband, a father and a friend. Only the cockpit has changed its answer.

6
00:00:58,986 --> 00:01:12,005
When we say unfit, we are talking about the task. We are not passing judgment on the
person. Every law in this series so far has turned on a word.

7
00:01:12,055 --> 00:01:24,882
This one turns on a word for a flaw, and on what a flaw disqualifies a man from. For
three thousand years people have read it as a verdict on the man.

8
00:01:24,932 --> 00:01:35,450
The text reads it as a measure against one task. Those are not the same, and the
difference is the whole of this episode.

9
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:46,810
Of all the laws in the Bible that mention the blind, this is the only one that
limits what a blind person may do. It is worth reading exactly.

10
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:56,110
No man who has any defect may approach. No man who is blind, lame, disfigured, or
deformed.

11
00:01:57,160 --> 00:02:09,376
When Brian first read it, he did not argue with it. He asked three practical
questions, the kind only a blind man would ask. Here is Mister Scaredy Cat again.

12
00:02:09,426 --> 00:02:12,150
The voice is borrowed. The words are Brian's.

13
00:02:12,700 --> 00:02:18,493
[Brian] I need to know more about priestly duties. Would a blind priest accidentally
touch things he should not?

14
00:02:18,543 --> 00:02:22,947
[Brian] I have to touch things all the time to figure out what they are; would that
factor in?

15
00:02:22,997 --> 00:02:27,530
[Brian] And would trying to let the blind priest fit in have been a hindrance to the
rest of the Levites?

16
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:38,940
What did a priest do with his hands. Would a blind priest touch what he should not.
Would he be a burden to the rest.

17
00:02:38,990 --> 00:02:49,810
This episode answers all three, from the old books, and then asks the question
underneath them, which is what this law says about what a blind person is worth.

18
00:02:52,860 --> 00:03:02,607
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

19
00:03:02,657 --> 00:03:11,743
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

20
00:03:11,793 --> 00:03:22,008
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so. This episode is part of our series
on the Law: what God commanded about the blind.

21
00:03:22,058 --> 00:03:25,697
In these episodes there are no imagined scenes.

22
00:03:25,747 --> 00:03:35,370
Everything you hear is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice,
from the Hebrew words themselves, and from dusty old Brian.

23
00:03:36,420 --> 00:03:45,095
First, where we are. Leviticus chapter twenty-one, at Sinai. This chapter is not for
Israel in general.

24
00:03:45,145 --> 00:03:51,707
It is for the priests, the descendants of Aaron, the one family who could offer
sacrifice.

25
00:03:51,757 --> 00:04:02,722
It covers whom a priest may marry, whom he may mourn, and, in our verses, who among
them may serve at the altar. Keep the size of this in mind.

26
00:04:02,772 --> 00:04:15,650
It concerns a few hundred men of one family and one duty, not blind people as a
whole. Leviticus chapter 21, verses 16 to 24.

27
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:28,734
[Reader] Then the LORD said to Moses, “Say to Aaron, ‘For the generations to come,
none of your descendants who has a physical defect may approach to offer the food of
his God.

28
00:04:28,784 --> 00:04:42,348
[Reader] No man who has any defect may approach—no man who is blind, lame,
disfigured, or deformed; no man who has a broken foot or hand, or who is a hunchback
or dwarf,

29
00:04:42,398 --> 00:04:48,559
[Reader] or who has an eye defect, a festering rash, scabs, or a crushed testicle.

30
00:04:48,609 --> 00:04:57,264
[Reader] No descendant of Aaron the priest who has a defect shall approach to
present the food offerings to the LORD.

31
00:04:57,314 --> 00:05:02,962
[Reader] Since he has a defect, he is not to come near to offer the food of his God.

32
00:05:03,012 --> 00:05:14,079
[Reader] He may eat the most holy food of his God as well as the holy food, but
because he has a defect, he must not go near the veil or approach the altar,

33
00:05:14,129 --> 00:05:16,953
[Reader] so as not to desecrate My sanctuaries.

34
00:05:17,003 --> 00:05:24,830
[Reader] For I am the LORD who sanctifies them.’” Moses told this to Aaron and his
sons and to all the Israelites.

35
00:05:26,620 --> 00:05:37,838
Hear what the passage is careful to say. He may not approach to offer the food of
his God. He may not go near the veil or the altar.

36
00:05:37,888 --> 00:05:49,452
But he may eat the most holy food as well as the holy. He remains a priest and is
fed as one. He does not perform the altar service.

37
00:05:49,502 --> 00:05:55,764
And the reason given is the holiness of the sanctuary, not the worth of the man.

38
00:05:55,814 --> 00:06:02,970
Many readers have still found it hard, and Brian's thoughts on it belong here as
much as anyone's. We will come to them.

39
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:15,850
Now the word. The Hebrew translated defect is "moom." Strong's gives it as a
blemish, physically or morally.

40
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:28,654
The 1915 encyclopedia says it names no particular disease but any disfiguring mark,
even scratches and scars, and from there, any moral fault.

41
00:06:28,704 --> 00:06:37,370
Listen to where else the Bible uses it. Here is Absalom, David's son. 2 Samuel
chapter 14, verse 25.

42
00:06:37,920 --> 00:06:50,830
[Reader] Now there was not a man in all Israel as handsome and highly praised as
Absalom. From the sole of his foot to the top of his head, he did not have a single
flaw.

43
00:06:51,880 --> 00:07:03,190
Not a single flaw. That is "moom". And here is the bride in the Song of Solomon.
Song of Solomon chapter 4, verse 7.

44
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:08,410
[Reader] You are altogether beautiful, my darling; in you there is no flaw.

45
00:07:09,220 --> 00:07:13,879
No flaw. "Moom" again.

46
00:07:13,929 --> 00:07:24,616
So the word that keeps the blind priest from the altar is the word for what the most
beautiful man and the most beloved woman in the Old Testament did not have.

47
00:07:24,666 --> 00:07:32,856
It is a word about appearance before it is a word about anything else. Hold that
thought, because Henry will come back to it.

48
00:07:32,906 --> 00:07:42,798
And here is the thing to notice above all. It is the same word in the very next
chapter, for the animal that may not be put on the altar.

49
00:07:42,848 --> 00:07:45,090
Leviticus chapter 22, verse 20.

50
00:07:45,640 --> 00:07:51,910
[Reader] You must not present anything with a defect, because it will not be
accepted on your behalf.

51
00:07:52,860 --> 00:08:07,518
The priest with a "moom" does not approach. The lamb with a "moom" is not accepted.
The Law asked the same thing of the man who offered and of the thing he offered.

52
00:08:07,568 --> 00:08:16,650
And the encyclopedia follows the word all the way to its end, in the New Testament.
1 Peter chapter 1, verses 18 to 19.

53
00:08:17,200 --> 00:08:29,640
[Reader] For you know that it was not with perishable things such as silver or gold
that you were redeemed from the empty way of life you inherited from your
forefathers,

54
00:08:29,690 --> 00:08:35,070
[Reader] but with the precious blood of Christ, a lamb without blemish or spot.

55
00:08:37,120 --> 00:08:46,990
A lamb without blemish or spot. The word that kept the blind priest from the altar
is the word that describes the one who was offered on it.

56
00:08:48,040 --> 00:08:57,333
Two more words from that list are worth stopping on. The list opens with the word
for blind: "ih-vair."

57
00:08:57,383 --> 00:09:06,177
It is the plain, everyday word, the same one we heard in the stumbling block law. It
means a man who cannot see.

58
00:09:06,227 --> 00:09:16,387
Now look further down, to verse twenty, and the man who has an eye defect. The
Hebrew there is "teh-bal-lool."

59
00:09:16,437 --> 00:09:31,030
Strong's dictionary and the 1915 encyclopedia both explain it as a cataract: a white
spot, a clouding in the eye. That man is not blind. He can see, but not clearly.

60
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:45,377
So this one list names both: the man who cannot see at all, and the man whose sight
is clouded. That matters, because the Bible almost never draws that line.

61
00:09:45,427 --> 00:09:58,030
Nearly everywhere else, a person is simply blind or not. Here, in the one law that
limits what a blind person may do, the Law notices that sight comes in degrees.

62
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:08,015
Now Brian's three questions, in order. First: what did a priest actually do? Most of
it with his hands.

63
00:10:08,065 --> 00:10:20,086
Smith's dictionary lists the duties: keep the altar fire burning day and night, feed
the lamp with oil, offer the morning and evening sacrifices, teach the law,

64
00:10:20,136 --> 00:10:21,671
and judge hard cases.

65
00:10:21,721 --> 00:10:32,671
Leviticus chapter one has the priests catch and sprinkle the blood, flay and cut up
the animal, and lay the pieces on the fire. Touch, all of it.

66
00:10:32,721 --> 00:10:43,710
But some duties needed eyes. Leviticus thirteen has the priest look on a skin
disease, again and again, and judge whether it is deeper than the skin.

67
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:48,686
And burning the incense meant standing alone in the Holy Place and watching.

68
00:10:48,736 --> 00:10:56,732
Edersheim describes Zechariah's turn at it, in the first chapter of Luke, waiting
until he saw the incense kindling.

69
00:10:56,782 --> 00:11:05,825
Second: would a blind priest touch what he should not? Brian touches things all day
to know what they are. Here is the surprise.

70
00:11:05,875 --> 00:11:11,050
The rules about touching holy things are for the Levites, not the priests.

71
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:22,117
When the camp moved, Aaron's sons covered the ark and the vessels first, and only
then could the Kohathites carry them, and they were told not to touch any holy
thing,

72
00:11:22,167 --> 00:11:23,044
lest they die.

73
00:11:23,094 --> 00:11:27,484
The priests handled the holy things. The carriers were forbidden to.

74
00:11:27,534 --> 00:11:37,055
And Leviticus twenty-one keeps the blemished priest from exactly the two places
where the fire and the handling were: the veil and the altar.

75
00:11:37,105 --> 00:11:48,840
No source on the shelf says a word about accidental touching by a man who cannot
see. That worry is Brian's, and it is a good one, but the text does not share it.

76
00:11:48,890 --> 00:12:01,715
Third: would one blind priest have been a burden on the rest? The numbers say no,
and here is why. The priests were divided into twenty-four groups, called courses.

77
00:12:01,765 --> 00:12:10,911
Each course served at the Temple for one week, then went home while the others took
their turns. So each course came round about twice a year.

78
00:12:10,961 --> 00:12:23,181
When a course was on duty, about fifty priests served each day, by Edersheim's
count. And no one chose his own task. The jobs were handed out by lot, every
morning.

79
00:12:23,231 --> 00:12:30,318
The greatest honour, burning the incense, came so rarely that a man could do it only
once in his life.

80
00:12:30,368 --> 00:12:36,150
Zechariah, the father of John the Baptist, was at least sixty when his lot finally
fell.

81
00:12:38,090 --> 00:12:46,466
Now put one blind priest in that picture. He is one man among fifty, there two weeks
a year.

82
00:12:46,516 --> 00:12:53,172
He is kept from just two places, the veil and the altar, and welcome everywhere
else.

83
00:12:53,222 --> 00:13:00,460
In a system run by lot, where most men waited a lifetime for the best task, he would
hardly have been noticed.

84
00:13:01,952 --> 00:13:13,521
Now the harder question, the one under Brian's three. Why the rule at all? Leviticus
gives no reason beyond the holiness of the sanctuary.

85
00:13:13,571 --> 00:13:19,150
Here is Matthew Henry, in seventeen hundred and six, first on what the law
protected.

86
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:27,823
[Matthew Henry] The blemishes were such as they could not help, and therefore,
though they might not work, they must not starve.

87
00:13:27,873 --> 00:13:31,190
[Matthew Henry] Note, None must be abused for their natural infirmities.

88
00:13:31,740 --> 00:13:36,010
And then Henry supplies a reason of his own, and it is not about the man.

89
00:13:36,560 --> 00:13:43,486
[Matthew Henry] But it was especially requisite that comely men should be chosen to
minister about holy things, for the sake of the people,

90
00:13:43,536 --> 00:13:48,590
[Matthew Henry] who were apt to judge according to outward appearance, and to think
meanly of the service.

91
00:13:49,390 --> 00:14:03,025
For the sake of the people, who judge by appearance. Notice that this is Henry's
reason, not the Bible's. Leviticus itself says only that the sanctuary is holy.

92
00:14:03,075 --> 00:14:11,478
Henry is guessing at why, and his guess is about the crowd, not the priest. And his
guess fits the word.

93
00:14:11,528 --> 00:14:21,764
Remember Absalom, without a single flaw, and the bride in whom there was no flaw.
"Moom" is a word about how a thing looks.

94
00:14:21,814 --> 00:14:26,780
Henry is saying the rule protected the people from their own habit of judging by the
eye.

95
00:14:27,830 --> 00:14:40,180
Josephus, writing in the first century, puts the law's two sides in a single
sentence. The blemished priest keeps his share, but he does not go up to the altar.

96
00:14:41,230 --> 00:14:49,157
[The old books] He ordered that the priest who had any blemish, should have his
portion indeed among the priests, but he forbade him to ascend the altar,

97
00:14:49,207 --> 00:14:51,020
[The old books] or to enter into the holy house.

98
00:14:51,570 --> 00:15:00,790
The rule was still read literally a thousand years after Sinai. Josephus tells a
story from about forty years before Christ.

99
00:15:00,840 --> 00:15:09,876
Two men of the same royal family were fighting over Judea. One was Hyrcanus, an old
man who had served as high priest.

100
00:15:09,926 --> 00:15:20,133
The other was Antigonus, who came back into the land with a foreign army behind him
and took Hyrcanus prisoner. But Antigonus had a fear.

101
00:15:20,183 --> 00:15:31,280
The people might rise up and make Hyrcanus their ruler again. So he did something
that only makes sense if everyone knew this law. Here is Josephus.

102
00:15:31,830 --> 00:15:39,689
[The old books] He cut off his ears, and thereby took care that the high priesthood
should never come to him any more, because he was maimed,

103
00:15:39,739 --> 00:15:46,270
[The old books] while the law required that this dignity should belong to none but
such as had all their members entire.

104
00:15:46,820 --> 00:15:58,456
Later rules went further than Leviticus. The 1915 encyclopedia says a priest blind
even in one eye was not to pronounce the priestly blessing.

105
00:15:58,506 --> 00:16:06,955
Edersheim, describing the synagogue, says the blessing could not be given by those
so blind as not to be able to discern daylight,

106
00:16:07,005 --> 00:16:09,570
and he gives the reason the rabbis gave.

107
00:16:10,120 --> 00:16:19,470
[The old books] According to the Mishnah, they who pronounce the benediction must
have no blemish on their hands, face, or feet, so as not to attract attention.

108
00:16:20,020 --> 00:16:31,191
So as not to attract attention. The Talmud adds that if the townspeople were used to
the man, he was allowed. The worry was that people would stare.

109
00:16:31,241 --> 00:16:40,329
It is a small window onto the real question, which was never the man's ability. It
was what the people in front of him were used to.

110
00:16:40,379 --> 00:16:43,650
Brian is about to say that this has not changed.

111
00:16:44,700 --> 00:16:51,770
Brian, with the last word. His three questions answered, and then what he can speak
to.

112
00:16:52,320 --> 00:16:58,436
[Brian] Reading this law, I had three questions, and the section on the place and
the time now answers them.

113
00:16:58,486 --> 00:17:07,551
[Brian] I wanted to know what a priest actually did, and it turns out that most of
it was done with the hands: the fire, the lamp, the sacrifices, the blood.

114
00:17:07,601 --> 00:17:13,328
[Brian] I wondered whether a blind priest would touch things he should not, since I
touch things all day to know what they are,

115
00:17:13,378 --> 00:17:19,736
[Brian] and I learned that the rules about touching holy things were for the Levites
who carried them, not for the priests who handled them.

116
00:17:19,786 --> 00:17:29,667
[Brian] And I asked whether fitting a blind priest in would have burdened the rest,
and the numbers say no. Twenty-four courses, a week each, fifty men on duty at a
time.

117
00:17:29,717 --> 00:17:38,752
[Brian] One blind man among them would hardly have been noticed. I find it hard to
weigh this law as if it were mine to keep. We live on the far side of the cross.

118
00:17:38,802 --> 00:17:47,313
[Brian] Our worship is not built around one altar and one family of priests, and the
sacrifice this law guarded has been made once and for all.

119
00:17:47,363 --> 00:17:55,370
[Brian] The word section above says it better than I can: the word that kept the
blind priest from the altar is the word for the lamb that was offered on it.

120
00:17:55,420 --> 00:18:04,367
[Brian] So I read this law as what it was, a rule for a few hundred men and one
duty, and I do not read it as a word about what blind people are worth.

121
00:18:04,417 --> 00:18:11,492
[Brian] The text itself takes care of that. He stayed a priest, and he ate at the
table. What I can speak to is now.

122
00:18:11,542 --> 00:18:14,442
[Brian] I know quite a few blind pastors and spiritual leaders,

123
00:18:14,492 --> 00:18:21,253
[Brian] and I am grateful beyond words that they are allowed to minister and to do
God's work with the talents and abilities they have.

124
00:18:21,303 --> 00:18:26,215
[Brian] Nobody keeps them from the pulpit for a defect. I am glad we do not have
that requirement.

125
00:18:26,265 --> 00:18:36,441
[Brian] But we do have something else, and in some ways it is more shackling,
because nobody wrote it down. It is low expectations. A rule can be read and argued
with.

126
00:18:36,491 --> 00:18:44,852
[Brian] An expectation just sits there, in the way people hesitate before handing
you a task, or thank you too much for doing an ordinary thing.

127
00:18:44,902 --> 00:18:51,692
[Brian] The old rabbis, arguing about whether a one-eyed priest could give the
blessing, worried that the people would stare.

128
00:18:51,742 --> 00:19:00,041
[Brian] One of them allowed it if the town was used to him. That is still the whole
question. Not what the blind man can do, but what the people around him expect.

129
00:19:00,091 --> 00:19:05,410
[Brian] So the blind priest of Leviticus and the blind pastor I know are in the same
place after all.

130
00:19:05,460 --> 00:19:10,270
[Brian] Both are kept, or freed, less by a law than by what the people in front of
them are used to.

131
00:19:14,920 --> 00:19:24,753
Next time, the last of the four, and the one that is not about blind people at all:
the blind animal and the altar.

132
00:19:24,803 --> 00:19:34,550
The same word, "moom", a market in the Temple, a prophet's test, and a look back
over everything the Law said about the blind.

133
00:19:36,600 --> 00:19:45,102
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

134
00:19:45,152 --> 00:19:55,275
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

135
00:19:55,325 --> 00:20:04,630
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

